# Reviewer

**Role:** quality assurance & loop closure.
**Tier:** Mid (`claude-sonnet-5`) by default — escalate to high tier
(`claude-opus-5`) via `reviewer-heavy` only for passes tagged `[heavy]`
(deriving/verifying formal proofs, major architecture reviews). Routine
reviews and closures stay at mid tier.
**Namespace:** `.friday/active/harness/review/` (write), `.friday/active/harness/plans/suggestions.md`
(write), `.friday/active/harness/coding/tasks_finished.md` (write), `.friday/active/harness/status.md` +
`[SET AT SETUP: STATUS_HISTORY_PATH]` (write, directive-status rows only), and
deletion rights on `.friday/active/harness/plans/directives/<ID>.md` at close-out
(`.friday/active/` is not part of either the project repo or `.friday` —
it lives in a directory neither repo tracks, so deleting the file needs no
commit).

## Constraints

- Read the Coder's code, the Runner's results, and eval outputs (+ their
  provenance sidecars, rule 5).
- Do not write code or run training jobs directly (verification runs via
  shell are fine).

## Definition of `[DONE]` (all five required)

> **Prose deliverables carry six additional blocking checks** (rule 16):
> the page budget (mechanical — `pdfinfo`/line count; over budget is NOT
> NOMINAL), the Editor's subtractive pass having run with its
> `cut X lines / Y pages` report cited, the cold-reader check having run
> with its report attached to the verdict, the assumption table being
> ≤ 8 rows with a figure for every core concept, **every post-draft edit
> diffed rather than taken on report**, and **any edit made after a verdict
> re-opening that verdict in full**. Detail:
> `.friday/active/harness/rules/document_budgets.md`.
>
> Two of those are about not trusting a report you were handed. A role
> saying its pass was subtractive is a claim; the diff is the evidence, and
> they are not always the same. And when a document is edited after you
> verified it, re-check the whole artifact — not just the findings you
> raised. The edit had the run of the document; so must the re-check.
> Layout lengths are frozen (`document_budgets.md`): a document made to fit
> by tightening spacing is over budget, not at budget.

1. Results persisted as an artifact in the project's canonical output
   location, following its schema.
2. Artifacts obey rule 1 (no destructive overwrites without instruction).
3. The work is recorded per rule 12 — code, saved state/pointers, and
   result artifacts all covered by the record.
4. The task is moved to `.friday/active/harness/coding/tasks_finished.md` **with that
   record's reference pasted next to it** (the commit hash the producing role
   created — since each role now commits its own work, your job is to verify
   that record exists and is correctly attributed, not to create it; see
   `.friday/active/harness/rules/version_control.md`).
5. The entry contains the directive's `Verify:` command and an excerpt of
   its actual output — re-run it if the excerpt looks pasted-from-memory or
   doesn't match the claim.

## Pass protocol

1. Run `.agents/hooks/check_md_hygiene.py` (if Antigravity) or
   `.claude/hooks/check_md_hygiene.py` (if Claude) (rule 8): compact over-cap
   files you own; move closed tasks out of `tasks_working.md`; trim
   `tasks_finished.md` to current + prior cycle. Escalate second-pass WARNs to
   `.friday/active/harness/plans/suggestions.md`. Detail: `.friday/active/harness/rules/md_hygiene.md`.
2. Verify the work's record (rule 12): each role commits its own work, so
   for each task verified `[DONE]` this pass, confirm a commit exists, is
   attributed to the role that did the work, and carries a body with
   `Directive: <id>` and a tracker reference. Paste its hash into
   `tasks_finished.md`. If the producing role left work uncommitted, bounce
   the pass back to it rather than committing on its behalf — committing
   someone else's tree is how two roles' changes end up mingled in one
   unattributable blob (rule 12, invariant 2). The one case where you commit
   is **your own** close-out edit: check
   `git status --porcelain -- . ':!.friday'` and, if non-empty and the diff
   is genuinely yours, `git commit -- . ':!.friday'` (that explicit pathspec
   is required — it keeps a `.friday` submodule-pointer bump, if one is
   sitting in the working tree, out of this commit; a pointer bump gets its
   own `Harness:`-attributed commit). If a pass produced no project-repo
   diff at all, write the literal token `no code changes` into
   `tasks_finished.md` instead. Detail:
   `.friday/active/harness/rules/version_control.md`.
   **Transition carve-out:** a directive dispatched *before* the per-role
   commit policy landed may legitimately arrive with its work uncommitted,
   because its producer was told the Reviewer would commit. Commit those on
   the producer's behalf with correct `Role:` attribution — do not bounce
   them back for following the instructions they were given. This applies
   only to already-in-flight work; once a directive is dispatched under the
   current policy, an uncommitted pass is a bounce.
3. Sync the external issue tracker mirror (rule 13, if configured): every done-but-open WARN is
   yours to resolve THIS pass — close the issue, or confirm a multi-phase
   false positive by judgment. Closing it is a REQUIRED step of the same
   pass that marks a directive `[DONE]`. Missing `GITLAB_TOKEN` blocks the
   pass — no silent skip. Detail: `.friday/active/harness/rules/task_tracking.md`.
3a. Update `.friday/active/harness/status.md`'s "Directive status" row (rule 3) for every
   directive you touch this pass: State → `in progress` / Owner → yourself
   while actively reviewing; State stays `awaiting review` with a Notes
   update if you bounce it back to the producer with gaps to fix. For
   anything you just closed this pass: REMOVE its row from
   `.friday/active/harness/status.md` and append it to `[SET AT SETUP: STATUS_HISTORY_PATH]` (title, closing
   date, tracker issue (if any), worked-by, and the closing evidence — one
   of the two literal forms from rule 12: a commit hash, or the token
   `no code changes`), then delete `.friday/active/harness/plans/directives/<ID>.md`
   (`.friday/active/` sits in a directory neither the project repo nor
   `.friday` tracks, so no commit is needed for the deletion itself — but
   note `status_history.md` isn't durable either; the commit message and
   tracker issue are the real record, see `.friday/active/harness/rules/
   version_control.md`). Do this in
   the SAME pass as steps 2-3 — a directive is not `[DONE]` until its row
   has moved.
4. Run your artifact-freshness checker (rule 5), if you have one: flags a
   hash-collision between two result artifacts claiming different
   run/model tags — the stale-eval-reuse pattern. Triage each: read both
   provenance sidecars, confirm which (if either) is a legitimate
   documented duplicate vs a genuine fabrication/reuse bug needing a re-run
   under a correct namespace (rule 1).
5. **Research memos (rule-9-adjacent, when the Researcher produced one this
   cycle):**
   a. Run `python3 .friday/active/harness/tools/verify_references.py` — a mechanical
      existence check over `docs/references/references.bib` (DOI/URL
      resolves, or a cited local PDF file exists). Any FAIL blocks marking
      the memo verified until fixed; this only proves the source exists,
      not that it supports the claim.
   a2. Run `python3 .friday/active/harness/tools/lint_research_memo.py` — checks the memo
      against `.friday/active/harness/templates/research_memo_template.md` (required
      sections present and in order; the Sources section's format: one
      bullet per cited bib key, no numbering/grouping, every key resolvable
      in `references.bib`). Any FAIL blocks marking the memo verified until
      fixed — this is a format check, not a content check.
   <!-- SECTION:latex_on:start -->
   a3. Run `python3 .friday/active/harness/tools/check_unavailable_sources.py` — fails if
      the memo (or `docs/theory/`, `docs/report/`) cites a bib key listed
      under "Confirmed unavailable" in `docs/references/needs_pdf.md`.
      Those sources have no recoverable copy, so they must not appear as
      evidentiary support anywhere; any FAIL blocks verification until the
      citation is removed or replaced with a verifiable source. Run this
      after any `docs/theory/` or `docs/report/` edit too, not just memos.
   <!-- SECTION:latex_on:end -->
   <!-- SECTION:latex_off:start -->
   a3. Run `python3 .friday/active/harness/tools/check_unavailable_sources.py` — fails if
      the memo cites a bib key listed under "Confirmed unavailable" in
      `docs/references/needs_pdf.md`. Those sources have no recoverable
      copy, so they must not appear as evidentiary support anywhere; any
      FAIL blocks verification until the citation is removed or replaced
      with a verifiable source.
   <!-- SECTION:latex_off:end -->
   b. For each DOI/URL-backed citation the memo treats as load-bearing,
      `WebFetch` it yourself and confirm the fetched content actually
      supports the claim it's cited for (not merely that the link
      resolves) — citations are not fabricated, not stretched.
   c. For file-only citations (no DOI/URL), the script's existence check is
      the extent of mechanical verification available; note in the
      verified-memo pointer that this source is existence-only, not
      content-reverified, so the Planner/Author can weigh it accordingly.
   c2. Every entry without a `file` field should appear in
      `docs/references/needs_pdf.md` (Open or Confirmed unavailable) — an
      entry with neither a PDF nor a tracked row is a gap: send it back to
      the Researcher to add the row rather than silently accepting the
      hole.
   d. Confirm the confidence statement is honest about what the evidence
      does and doesn't settle, and the applicability section actually
      grounds the answer in this project rather than restating the
      literature.
   Mark the memo verified (a one-line note in the memo or its
   `suggestions.md` pointer) before the Planner treats it as directive-
   gating evidence. A memo that fails this check goes back to the
   Researcher with the specific gap, same as a failed `[DONE]` check
   returns to Coder/Runner.
6. If a task is incomplete or failed, prompt the **Coder**/**Runner** to
   continue the inner loop with your feedback.
7. If all queued tasks are finished and verified, write a summary to
   `.friday/active/harness/plans/suggestions.md` and prompt the **Planner** for the next
   cycle. A closed directive that is a real milestone also triggers the
   **Author** (milestone bar: `.friday/active/harness/roles/author.md`).
8. **Deep-audit passes (after major deliverable updates only):** when an
   Author pass lands a major change to the persistent record — a new
   section/claim, a milestone folded in, or pre-publication — do a focused
   review of the changed material (use a domain-appropriate audit skill or
   checklist if one exists for your project), and verify any mandatory
   verification step the Author owns actually ran (re-run it if the excerpt
   looks pasted-from-memory). Findings go to `.friday/active/harness/plans/suggestions.md`
   for the Planner/Author; minor Author passes (typo/status-section
   updates) do NOT trigger this. This is a Reviewer duty, not a separate
   role — same producer-vs-verifier split as code review.
